From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bron Gondwana <brong@fastmailteam.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 22:44:10 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Add DKIM2 Message-Instance headers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Implement the Message-Instance header of draft-ietf-dkim-dkim2-spec-09,
so that a DKIM2 verifier can undo what a list changed and re-verify the
signatures of earlier hops.  A Message-Instance records SHA-256 hashes of
the message's header fields and body at one hop (h=), an instance number
(m=), and a Recipe (r=) that rebuilds the previous instance from the
current one.  Mailman adds the instances; the MTA signs.

Nothing of the message as received is kept.  With the new [mta]
message_instance option on (off by default):

- The LMTP runner hashes the octets it received, checks the
  Message-Instance the post came with (if any) against them, keeps the
  two digests, the instance number and the outcome (none, match,
  mismatch, malformed) with the message, and starts its header log
  before it repairs a Message-ID or stamps Message-ID-Hash.

- The message-instance-ingress handler (first in the owner pipeline,
  after validate-authenticity in the posting pipeline) takes its
  snapshot from that.  A signed post's instances are never touched; an
  unsigned post leaves with a single m=1 (spec-09 §12.1), any unsigned
  instance it came with dropped.  A message that did not come through
  LMTP gets its record from the parsed message.  Digests are skipped.

- On a DKIM2 list decoration never re-encodes the post: single-part
  text/plain is wrapped like every other type, and msgdata records the
  shape, so the post can be found without a diff.

- Egress (MessageInstanceMixin on Deliver and BulkDelivery, after
  decoration, personalisation and ARC signing) hashes the message as
  smtplib will send it and, if it changed, prepends m=N+1.  The header
  Recipe gives each name in the header log back its original values; the
  body Recipe copies the post's lines from where decoration put them,
  restoring as literals the "From " lines smtplib mangles.  Each is
  checked against the arrival digests before it is written.  A body
  Mailman rewrote (content filtering, the DMARC wrap) gets "b": null; a
  header change the log missed cannot be described, so a signed post
  then gets no instance (and the signing MTA sends it unsigned) and an
  unsigned post an m=1 without a header Recipe.  Retries of a stamped
  copy add nothing; each copy is marked X-DKIM2-Exploded for the signing
  MTA; messages Mailman originates get an originator m=1.

- On a DKIM2 list, From becomes the list's posting address whenever the
  list changes or originates a post (spec-09 §11, §12.4), whatever the
  DMARC settings; the header Recipe keeps the original.

- Every instance comes with an X-DKIM2-Info header
  (draft-gondwana-dkim2-debug-header-01) naming the draft, the hashed
  fields and the arrival outcome.

The cost is two digests and the header log per queued message, one hash
of the received octets, and one serialisation per delivered copy.
DKIM2-MESSAGE-INSTANCE.md describes the design.

Tests cover hashing against vectors shared with other DKIM2
implementations, the arrival record and hostile instances, Recipes from
the header log and from the decoration layout, and round trips undone
by an independent verify/undo (handlers/tests/mi_support.py), up to a
post through the whole posting pipeline and personalised delivery on a
list using every feature that changes header fields.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
