@@ -0,0 +1,323 @@
+# DKIM2 Message-Instance Support
+
+This documents the changes that add DKIM2 Message-Instance header support
+to GNU Mailman, per draft-ietf-dkim-dkim2-spec-09.
+
+## Overview
+
+A DKIM2 verifier that receives a message from a mailing list needs to undo
+the list's changes (subject prefix, list headers, footer) so that the
+signatures of earlier hops can be re-verified.  The `Message-Instance`
+header is how each hop records what it changed.  It carries SHA-256
+hashes of the message headers and body at that point in the delivery
+chain and, for every hop after the first, a Recipe that rebuilds the
+previous instance from the current one.
+
+Mailman is responsible only for adding `Message-Instance` headers.
+DKIM2 signing (adding `DKIM2-Signature` headers) is expected to be done
+by the MTA.
+
+## Header format
+
+```
+Message-Instance: m=2; h=sha256:<header-hash>:<body-hash>; r=<recipe>;
+```
+
+- `m=` is the instance number.  The first hop to see the message
+  writes `m=1`; each later hop that changes it adds `m=N+1`.
+- `h=` carries the algorithm and the base64 header and body hashes.
+- `r=` is present when `m>1` and holds the Recipe: base64-encoded JSON
+  of the form `{"h": {<header-name>: [steps]}, "b": [steps]}`.  Either
+  key is omitted when nothing in that part changed.
+- Each step is `{"c": [start, end]}`, copying a range from the
+  current instance, `{"d": [...]}`, supplying literal values from the
+  previous one, or `{"u": [...]}`, supplying literal values as the
+  base64 of their octets.  A literal goes in `"d"` when it is valid
+  UTF-8 (ASCII included) and in `"u"` otherwise (a Latin-1 or EUC-KR
+  line, say), as spec-09 §5 requires.  Body ranges are 1-based
+  inclusive line numbers.  Header ranges are 1-based bottom-up indexes
+  among the current values of that header name.
+- Mailman writes every JSON key in lower case.  The test-side parser
+  (`mi_support.py`) matches keys without regard to case and treats two
+  keys of one object that are equal ignoring case as invalid JSON, as
+  does the arrival check.
+
+Header values are folded for RFC 5322: lines target 72 characters and
+never exceed 77, breaking at `; ` tag boundaries where possible and
+inside the base64 otherwise.  The parser strips every run of folding
+whitespace before use (spec-09 §2.12 and §2.14), so instances folded by
+other implementations parse correctly.
+
+## Hashing
+
+Following spec-09 §4, these headers are excluded from the header hash:
+Apparently-To, ARC-Authentication-Results, ARC-Message-Signature,
+ARC-Seal, Authentication-Results, Auto-Submitted, Delivered-To,
+DKIM-Signature, DKIM2-Signature, DL-Expansion-History, Message-Instance,
+Original-Recipient, Received, Return-Path, SIO-Label-History, VBR-Info,
+X400-Received, X400-Trace, and anything with an `X-` or `Received-`
+prefix.
+
+Each remaining header is canonicalised (unfolded, name lowercased,
+whitespace runs collapsed to a single space, whitespace stripped around
+the colon and at the end), the list is reversed and stably sorted by
+name, and the result is joined with CRLF and hashed.  The body hash is
+over the raw body with trailing empty lines removed and a single final
+CRLF.
+
+The hashes are checked against vectors shared with the other DKIM2
+implementations in `test_message_instance.py`, so Mailman is pinned to
+the interop result rather than to itself.
+
+## What Mailman does
+
+**On arrival** (the LMTP runner, when `[mta] message_instance` is on):
+
+- The runner hashes the octets it received (header and body digests,
+  as a verifier would) and checks the highest `Message-Instance` the post
+  came with, if any, against them: `none`, `match`, `mismatch` or
+  `malformed`.  The two digests, the instance number and that status are
+  kept with the message (`msg.mi_arrival`); nothing else of the received
+  octets is.
+- It starts the message's **header log**, before it repairs a missing or
+  bogus `Message-ID` or stamps `Message-ID-Hash`.  From then on, the first
+  time any code adds, sets, replaces or deletes a header field name, the
+  `Message` records every value that name had, in order (none when it was
+  absent).  Python's compat32 `email.message.Message` edits its header
+  list directly in exactly six methods (`__setitem__`, `__delitem__`,
+  `add_header`, `replace_header`, `set_boundary`, `set_raw`); every other
+  method that changes a header goes through them, so overriding those six
+  sees every change.  The log is an attribute, pickled and deep-copied
+  with the message and never written into it.
+
+**At ingress** (`message-instance-ingress`, first in `OwnerPipeline`
+and immediately after `validate-authenticity` in `PostingPipeline`):
+
+- The snapshot is the arrival record.  A message that did not come
+  through the LMTP runner (`mailman inject`, a message posted through REST
+  or made internally) gets one here, from the parsed message less the
+  `Message-ID-Hash` Mailman stamps on it, and its header log starts here
+  with that stamp recorded as an addition.
+- If the message has no `DKIM2-Signature`, it enters the DKIM2
+  ecosystem at the list: the list's MTA will sign it as i=1.  Ingress
+  adds nothing, and drops any `Message-Instance` a sender or inbound
+  milter added (nothing signs it).  The message leaves with a single
+  instance, `m=1`, which egress adds over the message as sent, with a
+  Recipe back to the message as received (spec-09 §12.1 lets the first
+  instance record the changes made as the message enters).  An `m=1`
+  over the author's message would put the author's From at state 1,
+  where a verifier checks it against the list's i=1 (§14.8) and
+  fails; From on the outgoing message is the list's (see "From on a
+  DKIM2 list"), and the Recipe keeps the author's.
+- If the message is signed, Mailman never modifies its instances: they
+  are covered by the `DKIM2-Signature`, and rewriting or dropping them
+  would destroy the evidence of a broken chain and let Mailman pose as
+  the originator.  Egress adds `m=N+1`.  An arrival status other than
+  `match` is logged as a warning: the chain may not undo cleanly.
+- Only the two digests and the instance number (0 for an unsigned
+  post) go into `msgdata['mi_snapshot']`, and the arrival status into
+  `msgdata['mi_arrival_status']`.
+- Digests are skipped.  They are not a single authored message.
+- On a list that has opted out of Message-Instance, the arrival record
+  and the header log are dropped.
+
+**At egress** (`MessageInstanceMixin` on `Deliver` and `BulkDelivery`,
+after decoration, personalisation and ARC signing):
+
+- Mailman recomputes the hashes, over the message as smtplib will send
+  it.  If nothing changed since the snapshot of a signed post, no header
+  is added.  An unsigned post always gets its `m=1` (with no Recipe when
+  nothing changed).
+- **Header Recipe.**  The headers as received are the current hashed
+  fields with each logged name given back the values it arrived with.
+  They must hash to the snapshot's header digest; then the Recipe is
+  computed from them.  A change the log did not see cannot be described,
+  and there is no null header Recipe: that is logged as an error, and a
+  signed post gets no instance from Mailman (the signing milter then sends
+  that copy unsigned), while an unsigned post still gets its `m=1`
+  without a header Recipe, which a first instance may leave out (spec-09
+  §12.1).
+- **Body Recipe.**  No diff: decoration records where it put the post
+  (see "Decoration on a DKIM2 list"), and the post's lines are copied from
+  there, so the Recipe is one copy range (around a header and footer part
+  added to a `multipart/mixed` post, one range per stretch of the post's
+  own lines).  smtplib turns a body line starting `From ` into `>From `;
+  such a line of the post is restored as a literal `From `, unless the
+  post's body digest says it arrived as `>From `.  (The message itself
+  cannot tell: with 8-bit octets in a payload, CPython's `BytesGenerator`
+  writes its `>From ` into the message, and the digest's mailbox write
+  does that before delivery.)  The rebuilt body must hash to the
+  snapshot's body digest.  If it does not, the body Recipe is
+  `"b": null`: Mailman rewrote the body itself (content filtering removed
+  or replaced parts, a filter report was attached, the DMARC mitigation
+  wrapped the message), or the generator did not write the post back as
+  it came.
+- A message that already carries an instance newer than the snapshot
+  is left alone: a bulk delivery that failed temporarily is retried
+  with the copy egress already stamped.
+- Every copy of a message that came through ingress gets
+  `X-DKIM2-Exploded: yes` (once, however often egress runs on it).  The
+  list sends copies to more than one address, so its DKIM2-Signature
+  carries the `exploded` flag (spec-09 §12.5); Mailman does not sign, so
+  this field tells the signing milter, which sets the flag and deletes
+  the field.  It is an `X-` field, so it is not hashed.
+
+**For internally generated messages** (welcome messages, notifications,
+anything through `VirginPipeline`): these never pass the ingress
+handler, so the egress handler adds an originator `m=1` when the
+message has no instance at all.
+
+### From on a DKIM2 list
+
+From must be Authentic for the domain that signs (spec-09 §11, §12.4).
+A list that changes a message, or that originates it in DKIM2 terms (it
+arrived with no `DKIM2-Signature`, so the list's hop is i=1), signs with
+its own domain.  So on a list with Message-Instance enabled the `dmarc`
+handler rewrites From to the list's posting address, the way the
+`munge_from` DMARC mitigation does (the original goes in Reply-To or
+Cc), whenever:
+
+- the message has no `DKIM2-Signature`, or
+- the headers differ from the ingress snapshot (subject prefix, List-*
+  fields), the body differs (a handler rewrote it, or smtplib will turn a
+  `From ` line into `>From `), smtplib will drop a Bcc, or delivery will
+  add a header or footer.
+
+This happens whatever the list's DMARC settings are; a list whose DMARC
+mitigation wraps the message still wraps it, which sets From the same
+way.  A signed message the list passes on unchanged keeps its From.
+Anonymous lists already replace From with the list's address.  The
+egress header Recipe records the original From, so a verifier can undo
+the rewrite.
+
+### X-DKIM2-Info
+
+Every `Message-Instance` Mailman adds is accompanied by an
+`X-DKIM2-Info` header recording the draft implemented, the repository,
+the date of the implementation, the software name, the action
+(`mi-m=1`, `mi-m=2`, ...), the arrival status (`arrival=none`, `match`,
+`mismatch` or `malformed`, for a post), and the count and ordered names of
+the headers that went into the hash.  It exists so
+interop problems can be diagnosed from the message alone.  The format is
+draft-gondwana-dkim2-debug-header-01: a DKIM2 tag-list with every tag
+followed by `;`.  It is excluded from the hash by the `X-` rule and is
+folded to 78 characters, only after a `;` or a `,`.
+
+## Configuration
+
+Enable site-wide in `mailman.cfg`:
+
+```ini
+[mta]
+message_instance: yes
+```
+
+The default is `no`.  When disabled, neither handler does any work.
+
+Each list also has a `dkim2_message_instance` attribute (default
+`True`, exposed through the REST list configuration resource) so
+individual lists can opt out.  Lists created before the migration that
+adds the column are treated as enabled.
+
+## Files
+
+### New
+
+- `src/mailman/handlers/message_instance.py`: hash computation, Recipe
+  computation, header building, parsing, the arrival record, and the
+  ingress and egress handlers.  Mailman never undoes
+  a Recipe, so verifying and undoing a parsed message lives with the
+  tests.
+- `src/mailman/mta/message_instance.py`: the delivery mixin, following
+  the `ARCSigningMixin` pattern.
+- `src/mailman/database/alembic/versions/a1b2c3d4e5f6_dkim2_message_instance.py`:
+  adds the per-list column.
+- `src/mailman/handlers/tests/test_message_instance.py`: hashing against
+  interop vectors, the arrival record, header Recipes from the log,
+  structural body Recipes, folding and unfolding, the null body Recipe,
+  and full ingress/decorate/egress/undo round trips for 7bit, 8bit,
+  quoted-printable, base64 and multipart bodies.
+- `src/mailman/handlers/tests/mi_support.py`: verify and undo of a
+  parsed message (Recipe validation and application, spec-09 §5), as a
+  downstream verifier does them, for the tests.
+- `src/mailman/handlers/tests/test_mi_roundtrip.py`: round trips through
+  the real list transformations, and a post through the whole posting
+  pipeline and personalised delivery on a list using every feature that
+  changes header fields.
+- `src/mailman/handlers/tests/test_mi_null_recipe.py`: a body-only
+  change produces no header Recipe rather than a null one.
+
+### Modified
+
+- `src/mailman/email/message.py`: the header log.
+- `src/mailman/runners/lmtp.py`: the arrival record and the start of the
+  header log.
+- `src/mailman/handlers/decorate.py`: always wrap on a DKIM2 list, and
+  record how (below).
+- `src/mailman/handlers/dmarc.py`: the From rewrite on a DKIM2 list
+  (above).
+- `src/mailman/config/schema.cfg`: the `[mta] message_instance` option.
+- `src/mailman/pipelines/builtin.py`: register the ingress handler.
+- `src/mailman/mta/deliver.py`, `src/mailman/mta/bulk.py`: call egress.
+- `src/mailman/interfaces/mailinglist.py`, `src/mailman/model/mailinglist.py`,
+  `src/mailman/styles/base.py`, `src/mailman/rest/listconf.py`: the
+  per-list flag.
+
+## Decoration on a DKIM2 list
+
+Upstream decoration concatenates a footer onto a single-part text/plain
+body by re-encoding it, letting Python's email library choose a
+Content-Transfer-Encoding from the charset (base64 for utf-8), so every
+body line can change.  Every other type it decorates without touching
+the post: a `multipart/mixed` post gets the header and footer as parts of
+its own, anything else is wrapped in a new `multipart/mixed` with the
+header and footer as `text/plain` parts either side of it.
+
+On a list with Message-Instance enabled, single-part text/plain is
+wrapped too, whatever `[mailman] decorate_plain_text_in_place` says, so
+the post is never re-encoded.  Decoration records the shape in
+`msgdata['dkim2-decoration']` (`wrapped`, or parts added to an existing
+`multipart/mixed`, and whether a header and a footer part were added);
+egress finds the post from that and the boundary, which the generator
+chooses when the message is first written out, checking that no line of
+the wrapped text uses it.  Lists without Message-Instance decorate
+exactly as upstream does.
+
+## Resource impact
+
+**Memory and disk.**  Nothing of the received octets is kept.  The queue
+pickles carry the arrival record (two 32-byte digests, a number and a
+status) and the header log, which holds only the fields Mailman changed
+(typically a few hundred bytes); `msgdata` holds the snapshot's two
+digests.  A site or list without Message-Instance carries neither.  There
+is no cache directory.
+
+**CPU.**  The LMTP runner hashes the received octets once (they are not
+copied when they already have CRLF line endings, as LMTP delivers them).
+Egress serialises the message once, the way smtplib will, hashes it, and
+finds the post's lines in that serialisation: no line diff, linear in the
+message size.  Bulk delivery runs egress once on the shared copy;
+individual delivery runs it once per recipient after per-recipient
+decoration.  Decoration skips the re-encode it would otherwise do.
+
+**Wire size.**  Each message gains one or two `Message-Instance`
+headers plus their `X-DKIM2-Info` headers, a few hundred bytes.
+
+## Limitations
+
+The Mail-Archive.com archiver (`mail-archive`) mails its archive copy
+through the out queue with fresh metadata: no Message-Instance snapshot.
+Egress adds no
+instance for it, so the instance from ingress no longer describes the
+copy as sent and it is not DKIM2-signed.  The archive copy is the post as
+archived, not a delivery; this is a known limitation.
+
+## Code structure
+
+`message_instance.py` keeps the library-grade code (canonicalisation,
+hashing, Recipe computation, header building, folding and parsing) free
+of Mailman imports and operating on `email.message.Message`, with the
+Mailman-specific glue (config check, pipeline handlers) below it.  When
+a standalone Python DKIM2 library exists, the first part can move there
+and the handlers become a thin wrapper, as Sympa's implementation does
+by delegating to `Mail::DKIM2::MessageInstance`.
